Selection Sort -> Name is Selection so what we select , we select  Minimums & Swap
==============

Swap at index 0 , & min index [0 to n-1]
Swap at index 1 , & min index [1 to n-1]
Swap at index 2 , & min index [2 to n-1]
              |
              |
              |
              |
              |
             n-2


code: import java.util.*;
public class practice{
    public static void selectionSort(int[] arr,int n)
    {
        for(int i=0;i<=n-2;i++)     n-2 isliye kyuki last element n-1 hai aur woh automatically sort ho jaayega
        {
            int mini = i;
            for(int j=i;j<=n-1;j++)
            {
                if(arr[j]<arr[mini])
                {
                    mini=j;
                }
            }
            int temp = arr[i];
            arr[i] =  arr[mini];
            arr[mini] = temp;
        }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {5,3,4,1,2,6};
        int n = arr.length;
        selectionSort(arr,n);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));
    }
}

Best , Worst , Average Time Complexity : O(n^2)

Bubble Sort : Opposite of Selection Sort 
============
Push the maximum to the last by adjacent swaps 

    0 to n-1
    0 to n-2
    0 to n-3 
    0 to n-4 
    0 to n-5
       |
       |
       |
    0 to 1


code: import java.util.*;
public class practice{
    public static void bubbleSort(int[] arr,int n)
    {
        for(int i=n-1;i>=1;i--)
        {
            for(int j=0;j<=i-1;j++)
            {
                if(arr[j]>arr[j+1])
                {
                    int temp = arr[j];
                    arr[j] =  arr[j+1];
                    arr[j+1] = temp;
                }
            }

        }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {5,3,4,1,2,6};
        int n = arr.length;
        bubbleSort(arr,n);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));
    }
}

Time Complexity : O(n^2) for Worst , Average Case

For Best Case : O(n) if array is already sorted

